Liftup Pattern
In contrast to the liftdown pattern,the pattern that lifts up a part of component that depends on the state can prevents unnecessary rendering.
An example below shows &lt;SuperSlowComponent/&gt; when hide is typed in the form.



import { useState } from "react";

function SuperSlowComponent() {
  const now = performance.now();
  while (performance.now() - now &lt; 200) {}
  return &lt;div&gt;Super slow component&lt;/div&gt;;
}

function Form({ name, setName }) {
  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;label htmlFor="name"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input
        id="name"
        type="text"
        value={name}
        onChange={(e) =&gt; setName(e.target.value)}
      /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

export default function App() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");
  return (
    &lt;div className="app"&gt;
      &lt;Form name={name} setName={setName} /&gt;
      {name !== "hide" &amp;&amp; &lt;SuperSlowComponent /&gt;}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}







This method invokes rerendering the slow component everytime you type values in the field. For now, let's go back to managing useState in the &lt;Form /&gt; component instead of calling it from &lt;App /&gt;.

By using liftup pattern, The difference from liftdown pattern is passing the slow component as a children. Since children is simply a prop, it is not affected by changes in state and therefore will not be re-rendered.



import { useState } from "react";

function SuperSlowComponent() {
  const now = performance.now();
  while (performance.now() - now &lt; 200) {}
  return &lt;div&gt;Super slow component&lt;/div&gt;;
}

function Form({ children }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;label htmlFor="name"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input
        id="name"
        type="text"
        value={name}
        onChange={(e) =&gt; setName(e.target.value)}
      /&gt;
      {name !== "hide" &amp;&amp; children}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

export default function App() {
  return (
    &lt;Form&gt;
      &lt;SuperSlowComponent /&gt;
    &lt;/Form&gt;
  );
}







As a variation of the lift-up pattern, there is also a pattern where components are passed as Props instead of being passed as children.



import { useState } from "react";

function SuperSlowComponent() {
  const now = performance.now();
  while (performance.now() - now &lt; 200) {}
  return &lt;div&gt;Super slow component&lt;/div&gt;;
}

function Form({ left, right }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");
  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      {left}
      &lt;label htmlFor="name"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input
        id="name"
        type="text"
        value={name}
        onChange={(e) =&gt; setName(e.target.value)}
      /&gt;
      {right}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

export default function App() {
  return (
    &lt;div className="app"&gt;
      &lt;Form right={&lt;SuperSlowComponent /&gt;} left={&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}



  How to Monitor Your SvelteKit Application with Vigilmon


SvelteKit is gaining rapid adoption as a full-stack Svelte framework — combining SSR, SSG, and client-side routing in one clean package. But production SvelteKit apps need the same uptime monitoring as any other web application.

This guide shows you how to add health check monitoring to your SvelteKit app and set up Vigilmon for continuous uptime checks.




  Creating a Health Check Endpoint in SvelteKit


SvelteKit's server routes make this straightforward. Create a health endpoint at src/routes/health/+server.ts:



import { json } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import type { RequestHandler } from './$types';

export const GET: RequestHandler = async ({ locals }) =&gt; {
  // Check database connectivity if you have one
  try {
    // Example with Prisma
    await locals.db.$queryRaw`SELECT 1`;

    return json({
      status: 'ok',
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      db: 'connected'
    });
  } catch (error) {
    return json(
      {
        status: 'error',
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
        db: 'disconnected'
      },
      { status: 503 }
    );
  }
};






For simpler apps without a database:



import { json } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import type { RequestHandler } from './$types';

export const GET: RequestHandler = async () =&gt; {
  return json({
    status: 'ok',
    version: process.env.APP_VERSION ?? 'unknown'
  });
};









  Testing Your Health Endpoint





# During development
curl http://localhost:5173/health

# In production
curl https://your-sveltekit-app.com/health






Expected response:



{
  "status": "ok",
  "timestamp": "2026-08-04T10:00:00.000Z"
}









  Connecting to Vigilmon



Sign up at vigilmon.online

Click Add Monitor

Enter URL: https://your-sveltekit-app.com/health

Set interval: 1 minute

Enable keyword check: verify "status":"ok" is present in the response
Add email/Slack/webhook alert





  Monitoring SvelteKit on Vercel or Netlify


If you deploy SvelteKit on serverless platforms:

Vercel: Your health endpoint becomes a serverless function. Vigilmon checks it every minute — if the function cold-starts slowly, set a generous response timeout (10-15s) in Vigilmon.

Netlify Functions: Same pattern. Health endpoint becomes a Netlify function. Add X-Robots-Tag: noindex to prevent search engines from indexing /health.



// Add noindex header to health endpoint
export const GET: RequestHandler = async () =&gt; {
  return json(
    { status: 'ok' },
    {
      headers: {
        'X-Robots-Tag': 'noindex'
      }
    }
  );
};









  What to Monitor Beyond the Health Endpoint





URL
Purpose




/health
Backend health, DB connectivity


/
Homepage renders correctly


/api/products
Critical API route works


SSL certificate
Alert 30 days before expiry







  Status Page for Your SvelteKit App


Vigilmon automatically generates a public status page from your monitors. Share it with users so they can self-check during incidents:

https://status.vigilmon.online/your-team




  Conclusion


SvelteKit is a fantastic framework — and Vigilmon is the simplest way to know when your SvelteKit app goes down. Add a health endpoint, connect Vigilmon, and you'll get instant alerts with multi-region verification.

Start free at vigilmon.online.



  How to Monitor Your Bun.js Application with Vigilmon


Bun is a fast all-in-one JavaScript runtime — bundler, transpiler, package manager, and test runner. As more teams adopt Bun for production services, monitoring becomes essential.

This guide covers how to add health check monitoring to your Bun HTTP server and connect it to Vigilmon.




  Creating a Health Endpoint in Bun


Bun has a built-in HTTP server (Bun.serve) that's incredibly fast:



const server = Bun.serve({
  port: 3000,
  async fetch(req) {
    const url = new URL(req.url);

    if (url.pathname === '/health') {
      return new Response(
        JSON.stringify({ status: 'ok', runtime: 'bun', version: Bun.version }),
        {
          status: 200,
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
        }
      );
    }

    // Other routes...
    return new Response('Not Found', { status: 404 });
  }
});

console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${server.port}`);









  With Database Check (using Bun's SQLite or postgres)





import { Database } from 'bun:sqlite';

const db = new Database('myapp.db');

const server = Bun.serve({
  port: 3000,
  async fetch(req) {
    const url = new URL(req.url);

    if (url.pathname === '/health') {
      try {
        db.query('SELECT 1').run();
        return Response.json({ status: 'ok', db: 'connected' });
      } catch (e) {
        return Response.json(
          { status: 'error', db: 'disconnected' },
          { status: 503 }
        );
      }
    }

    return new Response('Not Found', { status: 404 });
  }
});









  Using Hono with Bun


Hono is the most popular routing framework for Bun:



import { Hono } from 'hono';

const app = new Hono();

app.get('/health', (c) =&gt; {
  return c.json({ status: 'ok', runtime: 'bun' });
});

app.get('/health/db', async (c) =&gt; {
  try {
    // Check your database
    await db.execute('SELECT 1');
    return c.json({ status: 'ok', db: 'connected' });
  } catch {
    return c.json({ status: 'error', db: 'disconnected' }, 503);
  }
});

export default app; // Bun will use this automatically









  Adding Vigilmon Monitoring



Create a free account at vigilmon.online

Click Add Monitor

Enter your Bun app's health URL: https://your-bun-app.com/health

Set check interval: 1 minute

Enable multi-region checks — verify your app is up globally
Add your alert contact (email, Slack, webhook)





  Deploying Bun Apps for Monitoring


Bun runs natively on Linux. Common deployment patterns:



# systemd service for Bun app
[Unit]
Description=My Bun App
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ubuntu
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/my-bun-app
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/bun run start
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target






Set Restart=always so systemd restarts your Bun app if it crashes, and let Vigilmon alert you if a restart loop is occurring (response time spikes + periodic downtime).




  Monitoring Bun in Docker





FROM oven/bun:1 AS base
WORKDIR /app

COPY package.json bun.lockb ./
RUN bun install --frozen-lockfile

COPY . .

EXPOSE 3000

HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=10s \n  CMD curl -f http://localhost:3000/health || exit 1

CMD ["bun", "run", "start"]






Docker's HEALTHCHECK works alongside Vigilmon — Docker restarts the container internally, Vigilmon tells you it happened.




  Conclusion


Bun's speed and simplicity make it great for production services. Pair it with Vigilmon for external uptime monitoring and you'll catch issues before your users do.

Start monitoring your Bun app at vigilmon.online.



  How to Monitor Your AdonisJS Application with Vigilmon


AdonisJS is a full-featured Node.js MVC framework inspired by Laravel. Its first-class TypeScript support, ORM (Lucid), and batteries-included design make it popular for building APIs and web apps. Like any production service, it needs uptime monitoring.

This guide covers adding a health endpoint to your AdonisJS app and connecting it to Vigilmon.




  Adding a Health Route in AdonisJS


AdonisJS uses a controller-based routing system. Let's add a proper health endpoint:




  Create the Health Controller





node ace make:controller Health --resource






Edit app/Controllers/Http/HealthController.ts:



import type { HttpContextContract } from '@ioc:Adonis/Core/HttpContext'
import Database from '@ioc:Adonis/Lucid/Database'

export default class HealthController {
  public async index({ response }: HttpContextContract) {
    const checks: Record&lt;string, string&gt; = {}

    // Database check
    try {
      await Database.rawQuery('SELECT 1')
      checks.database = 'ok'
    } catch (e) {
      checks.database = 'error'
    }

    const allOk = Object.values(checks).every(v =&gt; v === 'ok')

    return response
      .status(allOk ? 200 : 503)
      .json({
        status: allOk ? 'ok' : 'degraded',
        checks
      })
  }
}









  Register the Route


In start/routes.ts:



import Route from '@ioc:Adonis/Core/Route'

Route.get('/health', 'HealthController.index')









  Using AdonisJS v6 (new syntax)


AdonisJS v6 switched to ESM and a new router API:



// start/routes.ts
import router from '@adonisjs/core/services/router'
import db from '@adonisjs/lucid/services/db'

router.get('/health', async ({ response }) =&gt; {
  try {
    await db.rawQuery('SELECT 1')
    return response.json({ status: 'ok', db: 'connected' })
  } catch {
    return response.status(503).json({ status: 'error', db: 'disconnected' })
  }
})









  Testing Your Health Endpoint





# Local development
curl http://127.0.0.1:3333/health

# Expected output
# {"status":"ok","checks":{"database":"ok"}}









  Setting Up Vigilmon



